Draw the startup marker in place so a wedged boot leaves it on screen.

startup_debug=0x1 prints one line per startup entry. The panel console is a
ring (sweet framebuffer console: "lines wrap back to the top instead of
scrolling"), so once the log is longer than the screen the newest line is not
the bottom one, and the bottom line sits under the display's rounded corner on
raphael. Reconstructing "where did it stop" from photographs is guesswork --
it cost a day.

This writes the marker with a carriage return, so every entry overwrites the
same console line instead of scrolling: the screen always shows exactly one
marker, the last one executed, and it cannot be lost to wrapping or clipping.
The low 16 bits of the entry's function resolve against the kernel symbols.

Enable with startup_debug=0x2 (bit 1); 0x1 keeps the old per-entry lines.

--- a/osfmk/kern/startup.c
+++ b/osfmk/kern/startup.c
@@ -401,9 +401,14 @@
 	while (cur < startup_entries_end && cur->subsystem <= upto) {
 		if ((startup_debug & STARTUP_DEBUG_VERBOSE) &&
 		    startup_phase >= STARTUP_SUB_KPRINTF) {
-			kprintf("%s[%d, rank %d]: %p(%p)\n", __func__,
-			    cur->subsystem, cur->rank, cur->func, cur->arg);
+			if (startup_debug & 0x2) {
+				/* In-place: one marker line, no scrolling. */
+				kprintf("\rM%02x%04x", (unsigned)cur->subsystem,
+				    (unsigned)((uintptr_t)cur->func & 0xffffu));
+			} else {
+				kprintf("K%02x%04x\n", (unsigned)cur->subsystem,
+				    (unsigned)((uintptr_t)cur->func & 0xffffu));
+			}
 		}
 		startup_phase = cur->subsystem - 1;
 		kernel_startup_log(cur->subsystem);
